The works it translates
-----------------------

playcanvas-quake3 translates the programs below into TypeScript, and changes them to run on the
PlayCanvas engine, in a browser, a desktop shell and Node. Its splat-maps/ folder translates parts
of ioquake3 into Python, to write and check the maps the port plays. The files under src/,
master/, scripts/, electron/ and splat-maps/ hold code translated from these works and changed,
from 2026 on, and the source names the file and line each part comes from, as file.c:line. Each
work is free software under the GNU General Public License, version 2 or (at your option) any
later version.

ioquake3, https://github.com/ioquake/ioq3
    Copyright (C) 1999-2005 Id Software, Inc.
    and the ioquake3 contributors named in its files

Quake III Arena, id Software's source release 1.32b, https://github.com/id-Software/Quake-III-Arena
    Copyright (C) 1999-2005 Id Software, Inc.

Quake II, id Software's source release, for its CTF grappling hook (ctf/g_ctf.c),
https://github.com/id-Software/Quake-2
    Copyright (C) 1997-2001 Id Software, Inc.

Unlagged 2.01, as XreaL carries it (code/game/g_unlagged.c), https://github.com/raynorpat/xreal
    Copyright (C) 2006-2007 Neil "haste" Toronto <https://www.ra.is/unlagged/>

dpmaster, https://github.com/kphillisjr/dpmaster
    Copyright (C) 2004-2010 Mathieu Olivier

One part keeps a notice of its own: formatShaderTime, in src/sim/shader-remap.ts, translates part
of fmtfp, from ioquake3's bg_lib.c, and that file keeps Patrick Powell's notice for it.


Quake III Arena's data
----------------------

No Quake III Arena data is included: no pak, map, model, texture, sound or shader script. The GPL
covers id Software's source code, not the game's data, which remains id Software's. To play, you
need your own copy of Quake III Arena.


